iOS: OS_Unix::get_entropy leaks a /dev/urandom descriptor per call (4.7/4.6); please cherry-pick #121764

- Reproducible in 4.7.2-stable (iOS export, iPhone 13, iOS 26.5.2).
- The same code is in the `4.7` and `4.6` branches. Fixed in `master` by #121764.

iPhone 13 (iPhone14,5), iOS 26.5.2, Godot 4.7.2-stable iOS export template, Metal.

On platforms where `UNIX_GET_ENTROPY` is not defined (iOS/visionOS, Android below API 28,
glibc below 2.26), `OS_Unix::get_entropy` in `drivers/unix/os_unix.cpp` does:

```
int r = open("/dev/urandom", O_RDONLY);
ERR_FAIL_COND_V(r < 0, FAILED);
int left = p_bytes;
do {
    ssize_t ret = read(r, r_buffer, p_bytes);
    ERR_FAIL_COND_V(ret <= 0, FAILED);
    left -= ret;
} while (left > 0);
```

1. `r` is never closed, on success or on the error paths, so every call leaks one file
   descriptor.
2. A short read re-reads `p_bytes` into the start of the buffer instead of `left` bytes at
   `r_buffer + (p_bytes - left)`. (`/dev/urandom` does not return short reads for small
   sizes in practice, so this is latent.)

mbedtls seeds through `OS::get_entropy`, so on iOS every `Crypto.new()` and every TLS
connection (`HTTPRequest`/`HTTPClient` over HTTPS, `WebSocketPeer` over wss) leaks one
descriptor. iOS limits an app to 256 open descriptors. A game that polls a server over
HTTPS reaches it in a long session; after that, sockets, file opens and resource loads fail
(in our case a null texture crashed the app).

Measured on the iPhone 13 with a descriptor counter (fcntl over 0..getdtablesize()):
idling in town, open descriptors went 10 → 90 in 28 minutes, all of the growth
`/dev/urandom`. With `get_entropy` replaced by `arc4random_buf`, the same build ran 5.6 hours
flat at 10-12 descriptors. macOS desktop never shows it because macOS defines
`UNIX_GET_ENTROPY`.

#121764 already fixes this on master (Apple embedded uses `SecRandomCopyBytes`, Android
`arc4random_buf`), but it is milestoned 4.8 as an enhancement. Because the old path is a
resource leak that ends in a crash on iOS, please consider cherry-picking it to 4.7 (and 4.6),
or at least a minimal fix of the fallback for the stable branches:

```
int r = open("/dev/urandom", O_RDONLY | O_CLOEXEC);
ERR_FAIL_COND_V(r < 0, FAILED);
int left = p_bytes;
int ofs = 0;
while (left > 0) {
    ssize_t ret = read(r, r_buffer + ofs, left);
    if (ret <= 0) {
        close(r);
        ERR_FAIL_V(FAILED);
    }
[... 4 more lines]
```

1. Export the MRP below to iOS (4.7.2-stable) and run it on a device from Xcode.
2. Watch the log: the open-descriptor count rises by one per `Crypto.new()`.

One scene with this script:

```
extends Node

func _ready() -> void:
    for i in 300:
        var c := Crypto.new()
        c.generate_random_bytes(8)
        if i % 50 == 0:
            print("created ", i, " Crypto objects")
```

Each iteration leaks one descriptor on iOS, so it passes the 256 limit before the loop ends
and later file and socket opens fail; on macOS desktop nothing leaks. Xcode's Instruments (File Activity) or `lsof`
against the device process shows the `/dev/urandom` descriptors.
